**Known for**: maintainer of GNU emacs

Eli has been the maintainer of GNU emacs since roughly 2016, a position he
currently shares with Stefan Kangas and Andrea Corallo. In 2023, Eli received
the Free Software Foundation Award for the Advancement of Free Software, and on
the whole he largely conducts his maintainership work diligently and without
controversey.

Until late 2026, that is, when a discussion about the use of LLMs to contribute
to emacs was brought forward for discussion on the emacs mailing lists. A
participant in that discussion posed to Eli a hypothetical: "if i live in a
territory that permits slavery, and by law i am granted sole ownership of the
products of that forced labor, and then i use that to create contributions to
gnu emacs, complete with copyright assignment, and it is technically correct,
and, above all, you, the emacs maintainers are aware of how this was produced,
would it be ethical to accept those contributions?"

Eli's answer was "Our answer must be YES."[[source]][0] He goes on to elaborate
that political and ideological "divides" have "nothing to do with software
freedom". Eli therefore easily earns a spot as a weird little guy for his
emphatic belief that free software not only can, not only should, but *must* be
built on slave labor, because taking a position against slavery would be too
political and therefore undermines the free software mission.

[0]: $link.url('https://lists.gnu.org/archive/html/emacs-devel/2026-11/msg00128.html').attrs('source')
